--TEST--
What the copy boundary refuses is a closure, not a method name: a class declaring its own `invoke` crosses and comes back whole, while a closure literal is still refused — `rule:classes/graph-copy` and `rule:types/callable-values`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 2's refusal is about what a value *is*. A closure captures a heap and a
// scope; a class that happens to declare a method called `invoke` captures
// nothing, and `invoke` is a name a program is free to use. The two are told
// apart by the bit `nvs_runtime::ClassDesc::is_callable` carries, so this case
// asserts the bound from both sides — the last thing that crosses and the
// first thing that does not.
class Command {
    public int $code;

    public function constructor(int $code) {
        $this->code = $code;
    }

    public function invoke(int $n): int {
        return $this->code + $n;
    }
}

// Every declared slot survives the round trip, and the method is still
// reachable on the way back: `rule:classes/graph-copy` never runs a
// constructor, so what comes back is storage plus its class.
var $back = Core\Serialize::decode(Core\Serialize::encode(new Command(7))) as Command;
echo "code=", $back->code, " invoke=", $back->invoke(1), "\n";

// The other side of the bound, unchanged: a closure literal is refused by
// name rather than degraded into a stub.
var $fn = fn(): int => 1;
try {
    Core\Serialize::encode($fn);
    echo "encoded a closure\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
code=7 invoke=8
Core\Serialize::encode(): a callable captures variables from the code that created it, so it has no meaning on the other side of a copy boundary
